|> Sub 
    title=Width to Height via Xorification

   
    |> Definition 
        Let $F=F(x_1, \dots, x_n)$ be a $k$-CNF formula on the variables $x_1, \dots, x_n$. Define the Boolean function 
        \begin{align*}
        g(y_1, z_1, y_2, z_2, \dots, y_n, z_n) := F(y_1 \oplus z_1, \dots, y_n \oplus z_n) \ . 
        \end{align*}
        The above definition constructs a depth-3 circuit for g with a top AND gate, middle OR gates and bottom XOR gates. Each OR gate depends on $2k$ input variables and can thus be transformed into a $2k$-CNF formula; those can then be merged and therefore we can express $g$ as a $2k$-CNF formula, denoted by $G$. 


    |> Theorem 
        handle=theorem-2-Xorification
        *(citation needed)* Suppose $\width(F \vdash \Box) \geq w$. Then $\size(G \vdash \Box) \geq \pfrac{4}{3}^w$.

    |> Proof 
        We will go the other way, assuming that $\size(G \vdash \Box) \leq s := \pfrac{4}{3}^w$ and showing that $\width(F \vdash \Box) \leq w$. So let 
        \begin{align}
        C_1, \dots, C_s = \Box
        \label{eqn-resolution-refutation}
        \end{align}
        be a resolution refutation of $G$. The idea is that we will hit (\ref{eqn-resolution-refutation}) with a random restriction that will satisfy each clause $C_i$ with probability $1-\pfrac{3}{4}^{|C_i|}$. By an averaging argument, there is some random restriction that satisfies _all_ clauses in (\ref{eqn-resolution-refutation}) of greater than $w$. The remaining sequencee of not-yet-satisfied clauses, all of which contain at most $w$ literals, will be (equivalent to) a refutation of $F$. Thus, $\width(F \vdash \Box)$ will be small. 

        To fill in the details, choose $\rho : \var(G) \rightarrow \{0,1,*\}$ by choosing 
        \begin{align}
        (\rho(y_i), \rho(z_i)) \uar \{(0, *), (1, *), (*, 0), (*, 1) \} \ . 
        \label{eqn-sample-rho}
        \end{align}
        In words: for each pair $(y_i, z_i)$, leave one of them unassigned and fix the other one to a random bit.

        |> Observation 
            Let $C$ be a clause over the variables $y_1, z_1, \dots, y_n, z_n$. Then $\Pr[C(\rho) \ne 1] \leq \pfrac{3}{4}^{|C|}$. 

        |> Proof 
            The _index_ of a literal $l$ is the $i$ such that $\var(l) \in \{y_i, z_i\}$. We can split $C$ up by index into $a$ pairs and $b$ singletons, i.e., write $C = D_1 \vee \dots \vee D_{a+b}$ with $D_j = l_j \vee l'_j$ for $1 \leq j \leq a$ and $D_j = l_j$ for $a+1 \leq j \leq a+b$ where the literals from the same $D_j$ have the same index and literals from different $D_j$'s have different indices. 
            Now the events $[D_j(\rho) \ne 1]$ are independent:

            \begin{align*}
            \Pr[D(\rho) \ne 1] = \prod_{j=1}^{a+b} \Pr[D_j(\rho) \ne 1] \ . 
            \end{align*}

            For $1 \leq j \leq a$, consider $D_j = l_j \vee l'_j$, for example $D_j = y_i \vee \bar{z}_i$. Then two choices for $\rho$ on $(y_i, z_i)$ out of four satisfy $D_j$, namely $(1,*)$ and $(*,0)$, and thus $\Pr[D_j(\rho) \ne 1] = 1/2$. 
            For $a+1 \leq j \leq a+b$, consider $D_j = l_j$, for example $D_j = y_i$. Then exactly one choice out of four satisifies $D_j$, namely $(1,*)$, and $\Pr[D_j(\rho) \ne 1] = 3/4$. multiplying everything, we get 

            \begin{align*}
            \Pr[D(\rho) \ne 1] & = \prod_{j=1}^{a+b} \Pr[D_j(\rho) \ne 1] \\
            & = \pfrac{1}{2}^{a} \cdot \pfrac{3}{4}^b \\
            & < \pfrac{3}{4}^{2a} \cdot \pfrac{3}{4}^b  = \pfrac{3}{4}^{2a+b} = \pfrac{3}{4}^{|C|} \ . 
            \end{align*}


        A clause $C$ with $|C| > w$ stays not-yet-satisfied with probability less than $\pfrac{3}{4}^w = \frac{1}{s}$, and thus, when we hit the refutation $C_1, \dots, C_s$ with our random $\rho$, then on expectation strictly fewer than one clause of width greater than $w$ remain not-yet-satisfied. That is, there _is_ some choice of $\rho$ such that _every_ clause $C_i$ from (\ref{eqn-resolution-refutation}) with $|C| > w$ is satisfied by $\rho$. 
        Let $C'_1, \dots, C'_{s'}$ be the remaining clauses, i.e., each $C'_{i'}$ is $C_i|_{\rho}$ for some $i$. This is a refutation of $G|_{\rho}$, of width at most $w$. 

        |> Observation
            For each $\rho$ sampled as in (\ref{eqn-sample-rho}) it holds that $\width(G|_{\rho} \vdash \Box) \leq w$.

        Lastly, we claim that $G|_{\rho}$ is in a sense isomorphic to $F$. Indeed, $G$ arises from $F$ by replacing $x_i$ with $y_i \oplus z_i$. Thus, choosing $(0,*)$ at position $i$ makes this $0 \oplus z_i  \equiv z_i$; choosing $(1,*)$ makes it $1 \oplus z_i  \equiv \bar{z}_i$; choosing $(*,0)$ makes it $y_i$; and $(*,1)$ makes it $\bar{y}_i$. Whatever we choose, $x_i$ is simply randomly renamed into $y_i$ or $z_i$, and with probability $1/2$ all its occurrences are negated. This process describes how we arrive from $F$ at $G|_{\rho}$. Clearly renaming a variable and possibly negating all its occurrences does not affect resolution width, and therefore 
        \begin{align*}
        \width(F, \Box) = \width(F|_{\rho}, \Box) \leq w \ . 
        \end{align*}
        
    Let's take a step back: we go from a formula on $n$ variables and resolution width $w$ to a formula on $2n$ variables and resolution size $2^{\log_{2}{(4/3)} w} \approx 2^{0.41 w}$. So if we have a width lower bound of  $(1-\epsilon)n$, we get a size lower bound of $2^{0.41 (1-\epsilon) n /2}$, the $n/2$ taking into account the fact that the new formula has twice as many variables. So a lower bound of around $0.205n$ is the best we can hope for. Can we do better?
           

    What if we replace $x_i$ not by $y_i \oplus z_i$ but by $y_i^1 \oplus \dots \oplus y_i^l$? We get an $lk$-CNF formula $G$ on $ln$ variables. But what size lower bound for $G$ to we get? What's the obvious generalization to sample $\rho$? For each $i$, 
    
    |> ol
        |> li 
            sample $j \uar [l]$ and set $\rho(y_i^j) = *$; 
        |> li
            sample $\rho(y_i^{j'}) \uar \{0,1\}$ for all $j' \in [l] \setminus \{j\}

    One part of the above proof stays the same: when replacing $x_i$ by 
    $y_i^1 \oplus \dots \oplus y_i^l$ and then hitting this with $\rho$, 
    we are left with some $y_i^j \oplus b$, i.e., either $y_i^j$ or 
    $\bar{y}_i^j$. Therefore, $G|_{\rho}$ is isomorphic to $F$ for 
    every $\rho$ thusly sampled, and  the equation 
    \begin{align*}
    \width(F \vdash \Box) = \width(G|_{\rho} \vdash \Box)
    \end{align*}
    still holds. What's the probability that some $C$ stays not-yet-satisfied? A single literal becomes satisfied with probability 
    \begin{align*}
    \Pr[\rho(l) = 1] = \Pr[\rho(l) \ne *] \cdot \Pr[\rho(l) = 1 \ | \ \rho(l) \ne *] = 
    \frac{l-1}{l} \cdot \frac{1}{2} = \frac{l-1}{2l} \ . 
    \end{align*}
    So the probability of not becoming $1$ is 
    \begin{align}
    \Pr[\rho(l) \ne 1] = 1 - \frac{l-1}{2l} = \frac{l+1}{2l} \ . 
    \label{eqn-lxor-single}
    \end{align}
    For $l=2$ this becomes $\frac{3}{4}$, just as in the proof above. 


    What about literals coming from the same block? Suppose clause $C$ contains $y_i^1, dots, y_i^d$, without loss of generality the positive literals. There are two possibiltiies how $\rho$ could possibly _not_ satisfy the sub-clause $y_i^1 \vee \dots y_i^d$: either (1) the * is sampled from the other $l-d$ variables $y_i^{d+1}, \dots, y_i^l$ and $y_i^1, \dots, y_i^d$ are all set to $0$, which happens with probability $\frac{l-d}{l} \cdot 2^{-d}$; or the $*$ is sampled from the $y_i^1, \dots, y_i^d$ and the others are set to $0$, which happens with probability $\frac{d}{l} 2^{-d+1}$. In total the probability is 
    \begin{align*}
    \Pr[(y_i^1 \vee \dots \vee y_i^d) (\rho) \ne 1] & = 
    \frac{l-d}{l} \cdot 2^{-d} + \frac{d}{l} 2^{-d+1} =
    2^{-d} + \frac{d}{l} \left(-2^{-d + 2^{-d+1}}\right) \\
    & = 2^{-d} + \frac{d}{l} 2^{-d} = 2^{-d} \left(1 + \frac{d}{l}\right)
    = \frac{l+d}{l} \cdot 2^{-d} \ . 
    \end{align*}
    For $d=1$ this becomes $\frac{l+1}{2l}$, as in (\ref{eqn-lxor-single}); for $d=l=2$ this becomes $\frac{1}{2}$ as in the $y_i \vee z_i$ in the proof above.
    We claim that, as above, the worst case (smallest probability of becoming satisfied) is reached when all literals of $C$ come from different blocks:

    |> Claim 
        $\frac{l+d}{l} \cdot 2^{-d} \leq \pfrac{l+1}{2l}^d$.

    |> Proof 
        We cancel the factor $2^{-d}$ on both sides and are left with having to show that $\frac{l+d}{l} \leq \pfrac{l+1}{l}^d$. This is true because 
        \begin{align*}
        \pfrac{l+1}{l}^d = \left(1 + \frac{1}{l}\right)^d = 1 + d \cdot \frac{1}{l} + {d \choose 2} \pfrac{1}{l}^2 + \cdots \geq 
        1 + d \cdot \frac{1}{l} = \frac{l+d}{l} \ . 
        \end{align*}

    So partition $C$ by index into blocks: $C = D_1 \vee D_2 \vee \dots \vee D_a$, where the literals from one $D_j$ have the same index and literals from different $D_j$'s have different indices. Then the events $[D_j(\rho) \ne 1]$ are independent and 
    \begin{align*}
    \Pr[C(\rho) \ne 1] & = \prod_{j=1}^a \Pr[D_j(\rho) \ne 1] \\
    & = \prod_{j=1}^d \left( \frac{l+|D_j|}{l} \cdot 2^{-|D_j|} \right) \\
    & \leq \prod_{j=1}^d \pfrac{l+1}{2l}^{|D_j|}  = \pfrac{l+1}{2l}^{|C|} \ . \\
    \end{align*}
    Plugging this into the same computation, we see that a formula $F$ on $n$ variables with $\width(F \vdash \Box) \geq (1- \epsilon) n$ turns into a formula $G$ with $n' = ln$ variables with 
    \begin{align*}
    \size(G \vdash \Box) \geq \pfrac{2l}{l+1}^{(1-\epsilon)n} = \pfrac{2l}{l+1}^{(1-\epsilon)n'/l} = 
    \left(2^{\log_2 \pfrac{2l}{l+1} / l} \right)^{(1-\epsilon)n} \ . 
    \end{align*}
    The exponent $\log_2 \pfrac{2l}{l+1} / l$ reaches its maximum for $l=2$, so values $l=3$ or larger will not yield a better bound.